			"snippet": "Disclosure Team, I request an internal review of the SAR response (MoJ reference 260803049) dated 3 September 2026. There&#39;s 133 houses about to be developed on this land and I am trying to raise",
			"body": "Disclosure Team,\n\nI request an internal review of the SAR response (MoJ reference 260803049)\ndated 3 September 2026.\n\nThere's 133 houses about to be developed on this land and I am trying to\nraise the concern that their title is defective. Please cooperate.\n\nThe response states:\n\n“the MOJ does not hold any personal data within the scope of your request.\nThis is because the files have been destroyed in accordance with the record\nretention and disposition schedule (RRDS).”\n\nThis conclusion is not adequately supported by the information provided. I\nask the reviewer to address the specific points below.\n1. Evidence that the records existed\n\nI hold contemporaneous records from the European Commission of Human Rights\nconcerning Application No. 14464/88, Buckler v United Kingdom. Those\nrecords establish that a substantial documentary record existed relating to\nthe underlying proceedings and the United Kingdom Government's handling of\nthe case.\n\nThe existence of that independent documentary trail is directly relevant to\nthe searches that should have been conducted and to the subsequent fate of\nthe corresponding Government records.\n\nI therefore ask the reviewer to distinguish between:\n\n   1. records which never existed;\n   2. records which existed but were subsequently transferred to another\n   department, public authority or archive;\n   3. records which existed but were destroyed; and\n   4. records which still exist but could not be located by the searches\n   undertaken for this SAR.\n\nYour response appears to rely upon category 3, but does not identify the\ndocumentary evidence supporting that conclusion.\n2. Searches actually undertaken\n\nPlease establish, insofar as recorded information is available:\n\n   - the file references or index references searched;\n   - the names and variations searched, including Buckler, Williams,\n   Frederick Buckler, Mary Williams/Mrs Buckler and Sion Buckler/Hywel Buckler;\n   - the relevant court case numbers or archival references searched;\n   - whether historical HM Courts & Tribunals Service and predecessor-body\n   records were searched;\n   - whether records relating to the 1955 possession proceedings were\n   searched;\n   - whether records relating to the 1962 Cardiff County Court proceedings\n   were searched;\n   - whether records relating to the 1974 proceedings, including the\n   enforcement proceedings, were searched;\n   - whether records relating to the 1986 High Court judgment and 1987\n   Court of Appeal proceedings were searched;\n   - whether records relating to the Treasury Solicitor/Government Legal\n   Department's involvement in the litigation were searched;\n   - whether records relating to European Commission of Human Rights\n   Application No. 14464/88 were searched;\n   - whether records relating to the 1988 enforcement/eviction were\n   searched; and\n   - whether any records concerning the attendance, identification, welfare\n   or safeguarding of children present during the 1988 enforcement were\n   searched.\n\nMy request was not limited to the original court files. It sought any\npersonal data relating to me, including as a child present at the 1988\nenforcement, contained in any records connected with the specified\nproceedings or the Government's handling of Application No. 14464/88.\n3. Evidence of destruction or transfer\n\nYour response specifically states that “the files have been destroyed in\naccordance with the record retention and disposition schedule (RRDS).”\n\nPlease therefore identify:\n\n   - the specific RRDS disposal class or rule relied upon;\n   - the version and date of the RRDS applicable to those records;\n   - the applicable retention period;\n   - the date, or approximate date, on which the relevant records were\n   destroyed;\n   - whether a destruction certificate, disposal schedule, destruction log\n   or equivalent audit record exists;\n   - whether the records were destroyed in whole or in part;\n   - whether any records were transferred to another department, public\n   authority or archive;\n   - whether any transfer record exists; and\n   - whether any surviving index, catalogue, register, file list, metadata\n   or other residual record identifies the files which were destroyed.\n\nMost importantly, please confirm whether the statement that the files “have\nbeen destroyed” is based on an actual surviving disposal record, or is an\ninference drawn from the absence of the files in current MoJ/HMCTS holdings.\n\nThere is a material difference between those two propositions.\n\nIf there is a recorded destruction decision or disposal record, I ask that\nthe reviewer identify the record and the authority under which the\ndestruction took place, subject of course to any applicable exemption or\nrestriction on disclosure.\n4. European Commission of Human Rights records\n\nI also ask the reviewer specifically to consider the records associated\nwith Application No. 14464/88.\n\nI possess records from the European Commission of Human Rights relating to\nthat application. They provide an independent documentary trail concerning\nthe underlying dispute and the United Kingdom Government's involvement.\n\nPlease therefore clarify whether the MoJ/HMCTS searches included records\nconcerning Government correspondence, legal submissions, memoranda or other\nmaterial relating to that application, including material involving the\nTreasury Solicitor or other Government departments.\n\nIf such records were historically held by the MoJ or its predecessors but\nsubsequently transferred elsewhere, please identify the receiving\ninstitution and any known archival or file reference.\n5. Conclusion\n\nI am not asking the MoJ to recreate records that no longer exist.\n\nI am asking for an accurate account of what records were held, what\nsearches were undertaken, what became of those records (destruction or\ntransfer), and what documentary evidence exists of their disposal.\n\nIf, following review, the conclusion remains that no personal data is held,\nplease provide the reasons for that conclusion together with the recorded\ninformation requested above concerning the historical files and their\ndisposal.\n\nPlease treat this correspondence as a formal request for internal review of\nthe SAR response.\n\nRegards,\n\n\nSion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)\n\nRoyal Signals Cyber/Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member\n\nOn Thu, 3 Sept 2026, 16:19 NSUKilo, <NSUKilo@justice.gov.uk> wrote:\n\n> OFFICIAL\n>\n>\n> Dear Sion Buckler,\n>\n>\n>\n> Please see the attached response to your request (our reference 260803049\n> - SAR).\n>\n>\n>\n> Yours sincerely,\n>\n>\n>\n> Operational Support - Knowledge and Information Liaison Officer\n>\n> National Support Unit | HMCTS\n>\n> *gov.uk/hmcts* <http://www.gov.uk/hmcts>\n>\n>\n>\n> [image: Picture 725930249, Picture]\n>\n> *Here is how HMCTS users your data*\n> <https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/hm-courts-and-tribunals-service/about/personal-information-charter>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n>\n> OFFICIAL\n> ------------------------------\n> This e-mail and any attachments is intended only for the attention of the\n> addressee(s). Its unauthorised use, disclosure, storage or copying is not\n> permitted. If you are not the intended recipient, please destroy all copies\n> and inform the sender by return e-mail. Internet e-mail is not a secure\n> medium. Any reply to this message could be intercepted and read by someone\n> else. Please bear that in mind when deciding whether to send material in\n> response to this message by e-mail. This e-mail (whether you are the sender\n> or the recipient) may be monitored, recorded and retained by the Ministry\n> of Justice. Monitoring / blocking software may be used, and e-mail content\n> may be read at any time. You have a responsibility to ensure laws are not\n> broken when composing or forwarding e-mails and their contents.\n>\n"
